hi there steve kaufman here again  i've been asked to do a video comparing  classroom study to independent or online  study of languages  the  simple answer is that i think the  difference is that  online or rather independent study is  much more effective and much less  expensive than classroom study  and i'm going to explain why but then  i'm going to explain sort of a condition  all right  the reason i think that is that in my  own experience classroom study is very  ineffective now obviously if you have a  one-on-one relationship with a tutor  classroom study of that kind one-on-one  can be very effective but if you're in a  classroom with 5 10 15 or in some cases  in  in asia 70 in a classroom i don't think  you'll learn much in that scenario  the reason is that a lot of the time  you're listening to your classmates who  speak poorly i see the same result with  my grandchildren who are in french  immersion  they've had everything at school in  french since they started school they  take their chemistry their social  studies everything in french and yet  they have a very poor accent because  mostly they listen to each other  if  and i've mentioned before that the  majority of kids in canada who are in  the regular french stream after 12 years  or 10 years can't speak  if the same amount of time had been  spent  listening to  and reading content of interest with  occasional review  of the basic patterns of the language i  believe they would be better off  even if you are in a classroom  i think the learning takes place outside  the classroom again unless you're in a  one-on-one situation but if you're in a  class with five or ten people to me  that's not an environment conducive to  learning so you  the the classroom may inspire you may  set goals and of course the classroom is  a tremendous social environment you meet  your friends  but ultimately if you want to learn in a  classroom environment you have to after  the classroom listen read write speak to  people  and do all of the kinds of things that  you can do without the classroom now the  condition is that probably today a  majority of people  aren't motivated enough or don't have  enough confidence or don't know how to  learn languages so that they need the  classroom because without the classroom  they would not do much so to that extent  the classroom has the effect of of  putting the subject in front of people  giving them some objectives giving them  some tests to prepare for and forcing  forcing them to do things but if you are  motivated enough  to  learn on your own  then i think the classroom is is quite  unnecessary now  learning on your own though is people  have to learn how to do that and i've  posted a new post on my blog  and i'll leave a link here in the  description where i talk about learning  on your own and i think you need to have  a mix  between exploring the language exploring  different subjects via the language sort  of grazing  on the one hand which is maybe 70 or 80  percent of the time  and some more disciplined approach to  reviewing  in a very basic rote um  way  the main structures and patterns of the  language  recognizing that you will forget them  and they relearn them and forget them  and relearn them but some degree of  discipline  some effort to knock off sort of basic  simple tasks  that give you the sense of satisfaction  that you did something  needs to be mixed in with a more general  listening and reading  so if you proceed in that way  then there's no question in my mind that  learning on your own  is more effective and much less  expensive than learning in a classroom  however  maybe it's only a minority of people who  are capable of doing that i hope the  number of people who can learn  independently will  increase and you might also with  reference to that thought look at my  blog post on the subject of independent  learning thank you for listening bye for  now
 

